\details{
\code{predict()} returns exactly what the package's prediction function for the
model returns. For models from \code{\link[=fit_time_series_gp]{fit_time_series_gp()}}, \code{newdata} are time
values and \code{predict()} calls \code{\link[=forecast_gp]{forecast_gp()}}; this includes every
state-space model with time metadata.

\code{logLik()} returns the log marginal likelihood
\eqn{\log p(y \mid X, \hat\theta)}: \code{\link[=log_marginal_likelihood]{log_marginal_likelihood()}} for exact
and state-space models and, for sparse models, the FITC approximate
marginal likelihood or the VFE lower bound. This is a
type-II likelihood, with the latent function integrated out and the
hyperparameters \eqn{\hat\theta} plugged in. Its \code{df} attribute counts the
hyperparameters estimated from the data: the parameters optimized by
\code{\link[=optimize_gp]{optimize_gp()}} or \code{\link[=optimize_sparse_gp]{optimize_sparse_gp()}}, and 0 for models whose
hyperparameters were supplied,
plus the mean coefficients when they are estimated. For restricted
likelihoods (REML estimates and vague coefficient priors) the \code{nobs}
attribute is \eqn{n - p}, as for restricted likelihoods elsewhere in R,
and such likelihoods are comparable only between models with the same
mean.
\code{\link[stats:AIC]{stats::AIC()}} and \code{\link[stats:AIC]{stats::BIC()}} therefore work, but comparing kernels
by them is a heuristic: it is not an ordinary maximum-likelihood
comparison, and the penalty does not account for the flexibility of the
kernel. Heteroscedastic models have no \code{logLik()} method, because their
noise function is a plug-in estimate rather than a likelihood maximizer.
For latent models it is the Laplace approximation to the marginal
likelihood, and \code{df} counts the parameters optimized by
\code{\link[=optimize_latent_gp]{optimize_latent_gp()}} or the mean coefficients estimated by
\code{\link[=fit_latent_gp]{fit_latent_gp()}}. For state-space models \code{df} counts the parameters
optimized by \code{\link[=optimize_time_series_gp]{optimize_time_series_gp()}}.

\code{fitted()} returns the posterior mean of the latent function at the
training inputs, and \code{residuals()} returns the observed responses minus
those fitted values. For exact models the fitted values are
\eqn{y - (\Sigma_\varepsilon + \delta I)\alpha}, which equals
\eqn{m(X) + K\alpha} without forming \eqn{K}; \eqn{\delta} is the
numerical jitter. For state-space models they are the Kalman-smoother
means. For latent models \code{fitted()} returns the posterior mode
\eqn{\hat f} of the latent function, and there is no \code{residuals()}
method, because the responses are not on the scale of \eqn{f}.

\code{summary()} collects the training size and dimension, kernel, mean, noise,
log marginal likelihood, optimization status, and numerical status, and
prints them compactly.
}
